title ALES42 — Lotus's three-band tone stack: bass, mid, and treble from two one-pole splits
prompt Continue the ALES recursion loop Lindy-first crux-first; approve my own design rounds and make progress before checking in; send each finished increment to origin and xykj61 as its own signed round; stop at custody gates; baton prin recur
think ALES41 landed the two-band shelf — bass and treble scaling ALES40's low band and its exact high-band complement. Yet the tone stack a keeper reaches for on a guitar amp or a mixing console has THREE knobs: bass, mid, and treble. The middle band (vocal presence, the snare crack, the body of a take) is the one a two-band shelf cannot touch. ALES41's own witness names this as the next rung. Chosen Lindy-first (bass/mid/treble is the oldest, most-used EQ shape, on every amp and console front panel) crux-first (the hard-but-tractable core is the mid band as an EXACT residual — M = x - L - H, defined so the three bands sum to the input by construction).
think Shape: lotus/stack.rye over tone + timeline. The low band and the high band each live in their own scratch Clip copy of the span (so the original samples, the mid band's minuend, stay intact until the combine writes them); tone.low_pass runs on one scratch at the bass crossover lo_num/lo_den, tone.high_pass on the other at the treble crossover hi_num/hi_den. Then per sample x = clip[i], lo = low[i], hi = high[i], mid = x - lo - hi, and out = saturate(divTrunc(bass_num*lo, bass_den) + divTrunc(mid_num*mid, mid_den) + divTrunc(treble_num*hi, treble_den)) — each band divided in i64, the three summed in i64, one clamp over the true sum. StackError = tone.ToneError || error{BadGain} (shelf's own fault set, reused).
think Three properties make the stack honest and witnessable. Unity is identity: all three gains one writes L + (x - L - H) + H = x byte-for-byte — reconstruction holds BY CONSTRUCTION at any two crossovers, even if a band saturated, because the mid is defined as the residual so the three always sum back to x. Orthogonal at direct current: a settled constant has L = x exactly (ALES40's proven DC reach) and H = 0 exactly (its proven removal), so M = x - x - 0 = 0 — neither mid nor treble can move a constant, only bass scales the still low end. And a boost saturates rather than wraps (a loud constant at bass 2/1 pins to sample_max, the wide-domain sum clamped once, ALES8's fader lesson via ALES41).
obs GREEN on the first build across seven cases: silence stays silence; unity on all three bands (1/1, 1/1, 1/1) at crossovers 1/3 and 1/2 wrote a six-sample input byte-for-byte (identity); a constant 1000 over 30 samples at bass 2/1 settled its tail to exactly 2000 with the first sample still in transient; the same constant at mid 5/1 left the settled tail exactly 1000 (mid cannot touch DC); the same constant at treble 5/1 left the settled tail exactly 1000 (treble cannot touch DC — the knobs are orthogonal); a loud 20000 constant at bass 2/1 SATURATED to +32767 rather than wrapping; an illegal low crossover (zero den) and an above-one high crossover each refused BadCoeff, a zero bass, mid, and treble denominator each refused BadGain, an out-of-range span refused BadRange leaving the clip untouched.
obs Witness tools/ales_stack_witness.rish GREEN; tame_style_check GREEN (bans clean, zero-assert ratchet unchanged at 11). Reused whole: tone.low_pass, tone.high_pass, tone.ToneError, timeline.Clip/saturate/splice. Two scratch Clips, each one stack copy (max_clip 4096 samples = 8 KB), local to the call. Purely local — bounded i16 PCM; no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate (each crossover sets a split over sample indices, not a frequency in hertz — a real time base is a later rung); every custody gate untouched.
loom witness=ales_stack_witness result=green build=first cases=7 bands=3 mid=exact-residual domain=i64-wide clamp=single reused=tone.low_pass+tone.high_pass+timeline.saturate
file lotus/stack.rye ALES42 module — a three-band tone stack scaling ALES40's low, mid (the exact residual), and high bands independently and clamping once
